fix(agent,host): name what holds the writer, and take the fence reads off it

A remote command that times out says only that the *caller* waited. The lane
that made it wait had no record at all, so "agent slow" could not be closed:
a job that ran for 38 s, a process that was descheduled, and a lock held by the
checkpoint all looked identical from the desktop log.

1. Name the job. Every writer job may now declare itself (`call_as`), and a job
   that holds the ordered writer for >=1 s writes one row:

     storage job slow: job=commit elapsed=38120ms queued=0ms

   `elapsed` and `queued` are deliberately both present: a long `elapsed` names
   a job that must be split, a long `queued` with a short `elapsed` names a
   process that was not running (sleep, app-nap, paging) or a lane held by an
   earlier job. The cut is the same 1 s the command-side diagnostics use
   (`SLOW_COMMAND_MS`), so the two files can be read against each other. The
   idle reclaim slice and the checkpoint are timed the same way, which is what
   separates them from a writer job: the checkpoint has its own thread and
   never joins the writer's queue.

2. Shrink what holds it, without touching an atomicity contract. The compaction
   pass read its source row and each event page on the writer; those are pure
   reads, so they moved to the read lane and the writer now only publishes and
   retires. The remaining long jobs were already bounded by construction
   (reclaim slices stop at 10 ms, `content::reclaim` works in LIMIT 16/64
   steps, compaction pages by COMPACTION_PAGE_EVENT_LIMIT, legacy import commits
   per session). `delete_many` and `prune_events` are single transactions on
   purpose — splitting them would change what a failed delete leaves behind —
   so they stay as they are and the new row will say so.

3. Take the fence off the writer. `session_presence` is the first RPC of every
   remote content command, and it was read on the ordered writer because that
   was the only connection when it was written (2026-10-18, #1186): measured on
   2026-10-29, four commands released together after 18–21 s because one writer
   job was running, and two had already hit their 30 s deadline. It now runs on
   its own fence connection — not the writer, not the two-worker read pool, so
   neither a long write nor a large history page can delay it. The connection is
   opened on first use, so a process that never fences carries no extra fd.

   The trade is ordering, not freshness: a fence sees every committed write but
   not one still queued. That is why the desktop now asks once more (150 ms)
   before treating `missing` as fact — `deleted` and `import_failed` are
   verdicts and are never re-asked. Identity stays read live; it is two indexed
   rows, and a cache would only be another thing to invalidate.

4. The rest of the read-only queries that were still queued on the writer:
   `typed_events` (+ its paged sibling), `import_records`, and the compaction
   scans above. `queued_receipt`/`has_queued_receipt_run` deliberately stay:
   they are the admission check that a prompt was not already queued, so they
   must not read behind an accepted write. `load_metadata` stays for the same
   reason in the other direction — its read may repair the history index, which
   is a write.

Tests: the row's cut and field shape; a fence read answering while a writer job
holds the lane, plus the mirrored assertion that the writer lane still waits for
the job in front of it; `get_session_presence` answering under the same hold
through the RPC dispatcher; and the desktop's recheck — `missing` asked twice
(`deleted` and `present` once). `agent/src/session` (625), `agent rpc` (786),
`agent_bridge` (391) and `application` (109) are green, clippy `-D warnings` and
`make check-docs` clean on 1.99.1.
